(i) **Adaptive radiation** is the process of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally radiating to other areas of geography (habitats).

Darwin observed it during his journey to the **Galapagos Islands**:
- he saw an amazing diversity of creatures there, in particular a variety of small black birds, later called **Darwin's finches**;
- all the varieties had evolved on the islands themselves from a common ancestor whose features were **seed-eating**;
- as they spread into different habitats on the islands, their **beaks changed** to suit the food available there;
- many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become **insectivorous** and others to remain **vegetarian** finches.

Another example is the **Australian marsupials**: a number of marsupials, each different from the other, evolved from an ancestral stock within the Australian island continent. When more than one adaptive radiation appears in an isolated geographical area, it can be called **convergent evolution** (placental mammals in Australia show it alongside the marsupials).

(ii) **No**, human evolution is not an example of adaptive radiation. Adaptive radiation produces many different species from one ancestor at the same time, each adapted to a different habitat in a geographical area. Human evolution instead is a **progressive, single line of descent**: one form gave rise to the next over time (Australopithecines, then Homo habilis, then Homo erectus, then Neanderthal man and Homo sapiens), rather than a common ancestor radiating into several species in different habitats.
